  "provenance": {
    "_readme": "How each field above got its value: 'inferred' (a survey or a skill proposed it — authoritative for nothing), 'declared' (a human wrote it), 'approved' (a human reviewed an inferred value and ratified it). Optional, and starting empty is honest: doctor.py reports every field with no entry here as UNTRUSTED and prints the ratified-of-total count on every run, so an empty block reads as '0 of N ratified' rather than as approval. There is no 'contradicted' state to write — doctor computes it when an approved or declared field stops matching the tree. Add `verifiedAgainst.commit` (a git SHA) when you ratify a field: staleness is measured in diffs, not days, and there is deliberately no expiry date.",
    "fields": {
      "_example": "Delete this key and add real entries as you ratify fields, e.g. \"source.root\": { \"state\": \"approved\", \"by\": \"TODO\", \"on\": \"TODO-YYYY-MM-DD\", \"value\": \"src\", \"evidence\": \"TODO: why this is right\", \"verifiedAgainst\": { \"commit\": \"TODO-git-sha\" } }"
    }
  }
